# Angular coding style

Narrows the stack-agnostic style rules to current Angular idiom (standalone
components, signals, `inject()`). Applies to `*.ts` and component
`*.html` templates; everything not Angular-specific still comes from the
common rules this file `extends`.

## Components and files

- Components, directives, and pipes are standalone by default in current
  Angular — do not set `standalone: false` or write a new `NgModule` to
  register one unless the project itself is still NgModule-based
  end-to-end.
- One component (and its template/styles) per file, named after the
  component class (`OrderSummary` in `order-summary.ts`, not a generic
  `component.ts`). Match whatever file-suffix convention (`.component.ts`
  vs. plain `.ts`) the project's own `angular.json`/existing files already
  use rather than introducing a second one.
- Keep a component's template under roughly 60-80 lines before extracting a
  child component; a template doing significant `@if`/`@for` branching for
  more than one concern is a sign the branches belong in separate
  components.

## Dependency injection

- Prefer the `inject()` function over constructor-parameter injection for
  new code (`private readonly router = inject(Router);`), since it composes
  cleanly with field initializers and works outside constructors (guards,
  resolvers, `provide*` factories). Do not mix `inject()` and
  constructor-parameter injection for different dependencies in the same
  class — pick one per class, and prefer `inject()` for new classes.
- `inject()` only works inside an injection context (a constructor, a field
  initializer, or a call wrapped in `runInInjectionContext`) — do not call
  it inside a `setTimeout`, a plain async callback, or after the
  constructor has returned.

## Signals and state

- Use `signal()` for local component/service state that changes over time,
  `computed()` for values derived from other signals, and `input()`/
  `model()` for component inputs — do not read/write a signal's backing
  value directly; always go through `.set()`/`.update()`.
- Keep a `computed()`'s computation pure (no side effects, no writes to
  other signals inside it) — if a change needs to trigger a side effect,
  use `effect()`, not a computed callback abused for its side effect.
- Do not mirror a signal's value into a plain class field "for convenience"
  in the template — read the signal directly (`value()`) so Angular's
  fine-grained reactivity tracks the actual dependency.

## Templates and control flow

- Use the built-in control flow (`@if`, `@for` with a `track` expression,
  `@switch`) in new templates, not `*ngIf`/`*ngFor`/`*ngSwitch` — unless the
  surrounding component's own template still uses the structural-directive
  form throughout, in which case match the existing file rather than
  mixing both styles in one template.
- Always give `@for` an explicit `track` expression (the item's own stable
  id, not `$index`, unless the list has no natural identity) — an untracked
  or index-tracked loop over data that reorders forces Angular to
  re-render every row instead of just the ones that changed.

## Naming and typing

- `PascalCase` for component/directive/pipe/service classes, `camelCase`
  for signals, fields, and methods — a signal's own name should read as the
  value it holds (`user`, not `userSignal`), since calling it (`user()`)
  already signals reactivity.
- Type every `input()`/`output()`/`model()` explicitly
  (`input<Order | null>(null)`) rather than relying on inference from a
  loosely-typed default; an untyped input silently becomes `unknown`/`any`
  at every call site.
- Avoid `any` in component/service code; when the Angular CLI's generated
  boilerplate produces a loosely-typed value (e.g. a form's raw value),
  narrow it explicitly at the boundary instead of propagating `any`
  through the class.
